  <h1>Work Scale Model</h1>
  <p>Scale determines how much process is appropriate — a patch and a new project must not follow the same workflow.</p>
  
  <img src="/Autonomous-Duck-Development-Framework/images/3_Work Scale Model.png" alt="Work Scale Model" />
  
  <h2>The 5 Scales of Work</h2>
  
  <h3>Project</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Project</strong> is the full product or initiative. It uses the full lifecycle: Research, Design, Architecture, Sprint Planning, Build & Test, Review, and Release.</p>
  <blockquote><strong>Rule:</strong> Scale controls process depth.</blockquote>
  
  <h3>Release</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Release</strong> is a versioned delivery target — a bounded set of features grouped into one shippable unit (e.g., v0.1 Public Proof).</p>
  <blockquote><strong>Rule:</strong> A new release updates the project brain. It does not restart the project from zero.</blockquote>

  <h3>Feature</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Feature</strong> is a user-visible or system-visible capability added to an existing project. It may require research, design, validation, and multiple sprints.</p>

  <h3>Sprint</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Sprint</strong> is a bounded AI implementation packet. It typically contains requirements, a blueprint, acceptance criteria, a dry run, and implementation logs. It is the unit that runs through the Sprint Loop.</p>
  <blockquote><strong>Rule:</strong> A sprint ends when state is updated, not when code compiles.</blockquote>

  <h3>Patch</h3>
  <p>A <strong>Patch</strong> is a small, low-risk change (e.g., fix a typo, update a link). It does not require the full sprint loop.</p>
  <blockquote><strong>Rule:</strong> Do not run a project process for a patch. Do not run a patch process for a project.</blockquote>

  <img src="/Autonomous-Duck-Development-Framework/images/4_Scale  Lifecycle  Mode interlock.png" alt="Scale Lifecycle Interlock" />
